# Python patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Python 3.12/3.13 patterns and the anti-patterns they replace. Applies only to
`*.py`/`*.pyi` files (W1-AC8).

## Data and typing

- Model a fixed set of related fields with `@dataclass` (or `attrs` if the
  project already depends on it) instead of a `dict` with string keys — a
  typo in a dict key fails at runtime, a typo in a dataclass field fails at
  lint/type-check time.
- Use `TypedDict` for a dict shape that genuinely must stay a `dict` (e.g. a
  JSON payload), not for data your own code constructs and passes around.
- Reach for `typing.Protocol` to type "anything with this method" instead of
  requiring a concrete base class when the caller only needs structural
  compatibility — this keeps callers free to pass any matching object.
- Use `Generic[T]`/type parameter syntax (`class Box[T]:` on 3.12+) for a
  container or wrapper whose element type varies by call site, rather than
  typing it `Any` and re-casting at every use.

## Control flow and resources

- Acquire any closable/lockable resource (file, socket, DB connection, lock,
  temp directory) with a `with` (or `async with`) block; never rely on `del`,
  garbage collection, or a manual `.close()` that a raised exception can skip.
- Write a custom context manager as a generator decorated
  `@contextlib.contextmanager` rather than a hand-rolled
  `__enter__`/`__exit__` class, unless the class also needs other methods.
- Raise a new exception `from` the one being handled
  (`raise ValueError(...) from exc`) instead of a bare `raise ValueError(...)`
  inside an `except` block — this preserves the causal chain instead of
  presenting the new exception as unrelated.
- Prefer early return over nested `if`/`else` for guard conditions; a
  function whose happy path is indented inside three levels of conditionals
  should invert its guards.

## Async

- Group a set of related concurrent awaitables with `asyncio.TaskGroup`
  (3.11+) instead of manually tracking a list of tasks and awaiting them
  with `asyncio.gather` — a `TaskGroup` cancels its siblings automatically
  when one fails.
- Treat `asyncio.CancelledError` as a signal to clean up and re-raise, not an
  error to swallow with a broad `except Exception` that would also catch
  cancellation.
- Never call a blocking function (`requests.get`, `time.sleep`, synchronous
  file I/O, a CPU-bound loop) directly inside an `async def` — use the async
  client, `asyncio.sleep`, or `asyncio.to_thread`/an executor instead.

## Iteration and collections

- Prefer a generator or generator expression over building an intermediate
  list when the caller only iterates the result once.
- Use `itertools`/`collections` (`defaultdict`, `Counter`, `chain`,
  `groupby`) instead of hand-rolled loops that reimplement them.
- Avoid a mutable default argument (`def f(items=[])`); default to `None`
  and initialize inside the function body — a mutable default is shared and
  mutated across every call that omits the argument.

## Logging

- Use the standard `logging` module (or the project's configured structured
  logger), not `print`, for anything beyond a throwaway script.
- Pass interpolation arguments to the logging call
  (`logger.info("got %s", value)`), not an f-string
  (`logger.info(f"got {value}")`) — the f-string formats even when the log
  level is disabled, and the lazy form does not.

## Anti-patterns to flag, not introduce

- Wrapping an entire function body in `try/except Exception: pass` to make a
  flaky call "just work" — this hides real failures instead of handling the
  specific one expected.
- Reassigning a loop variable's type mid-loop, or reusing one name for two
  unrelated purposes in the same function — confuses both readers and type
  checkers.
- Deep inheritance chains built to share a few methods — prefer composition
  or a `Protocol` unless the hierarchy models a genuine is-a relationship.
